{
  "state": {
    "case_id": "151",
    "case_summary": "A 33-year-old Hispanic man with no known past medical history presented with a 1½-week history of headache and blurred vision. MRI of the brain demonstrated a 2.7-cm ring-enhancing lesion in the right temporal lobe with mass effect. He underwent craniotomy with biopsy and resection. Postoperatively, he was found to have HIV infection with a CD4 count <20 cells/µL and an HIV viral load of 191 000 copies/mL. Serum toxoplasma IgG was elevated, and CSF studies showed 48 cells/µL (97% lymphocytes) with positive toxoplasma IgG and DNA-PCR for Toxoplasma gondii (286 copies/mL). Other CSF studies, including fungal cultures, cryptococcal antigen, and AFB smear/culture, were negative. Histopathologic examination of the brain tissue revealed bradyzoites and tachyzoites. He was treated with trimethoprim/sulfamethoxazole and started on antiretroviral therapy; empiric antibiotics for bacterial abscess were discontinued.\n\nTwo weeks after discharge, he returned with fever to 39.6 °C, generalized weakness, and a 1-day history of productive cough. Chest radiograph showed a new left upper lobe opacification with diffuse reticulonodular interstitial prominence. CT scan of the chest confirmed extensive reticulonodular interstitial infiltrates with focal consolidation in the left upper lobe. He was started on vancomycin, piperacillin/tazobactam, and fluconazole and placed on airborne precautions. His respiratory status worsened, and intubation was planned pending further diagnostic evaluation.\n\nWhat is the most likely diagnosis?\n\nOptions:\nA. coccidioidomycosis\nB. CNS toxoplasmosis was the leading diagnosis because of positive serum and CSF toxoplasma markers and typical imaging\nC. Primary CNS lymphoma was considered in the differential for a ring‐enhancing lesion but excluded after biopsy\nD. Bacterial brain abscess was empirically treated but deemed unlikely after negative cultures and definitive parasitic\nE. Tuberculoma was in the CNS differential but excluded by negative acid\nF. Fungal abscess (including Cryptococcus, Histoplasma, or Coccidioides) was ruled out on brain biopsy\nG. Bacterial pneumonia was initially treated given fever and new infiltrates but was less likely after lack of clinical\nH. Pulmonary tuberculosis was considered but unlikely after negative sputum AFB smears\n",
